00:02:43.065 --> 00:02:45.690
So we should just tie everything
together and make it all work,

00:02:45.690 --> 00:02:46.189
right?

00:02:46.189 --> 00:02:47.170
Should be simple.

00:02:47.170 --> 00:02:49.110
Well, unfortunately,
as many of you

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:50.610
may have experienced
if you've tried

00:02:50.610 --> 00:02:52.151
to implement these
kinds of workflows

00:02:52.151 --> 00:02:54.960
in the past, the making it
work part hasn't necessarily

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:56.569
always been so easy, right?

00:02:56.569 --> 00:02:58.110
In many cases,
especially when you're

00:02:58.110 --> 00:03:00.720
dealing with data and the
considerations around data,

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.450
you may have found yourself
implementing isolated storage

00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:05.680
islands in the cloud.

00:03:05.680 --> 00:03:07.800
You may have found
yourself pushing data

00:03:07.800 --> 00:03:11.970
into the cloud with manual
type of forklift migrations

00:03:11.970 --> 00:03:13.724
to manage the data in the cloud.

00:03:13.724 --> 00:03:15.390
You may have implemented
complex schemes

00:03:15.390 --> 00:03:17.820
to copy data between
storage islands

00:03:17.820 --> 00:03:20.280
in an effort to create some
semblance of synchronization

00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:21.810
or data sharing, right?

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:24.270
Now of course, these
types of manual methods,

00:03:24.270 --> 00:03:27.330
they definitely can work, but
they can be extremely painful.

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:29.040
Fortunately, with
what Kubernetes

00:03:29.040 --> 00:03:31.830
has brought to the table,
and with the data management

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:34.288
technologies and techniques
that I'm going to be describing

00:03:34.288 --> 00:03:36.590
and that we'll be demoing
today, implementing

00:03:36.590 --> 00:03:41.180
these sorts of workflows
is easier than ever before.

00:03:41.180 --> 00:03:43.360
Now, before we get into
the implementation and some

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:44.860
of the specifics
around that, I want

00:03:44.860 --> 00:03:47.270
to take a quick step back
and see how we got here.

00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:49.570
So as you all know, right--
no surprise-- containers

00:03:49.570 --> 00:03:50.410
are everywhere.

00:03:50.410 --> 00:03:52.450
At this conference, at
almost any conference

00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:55.450
in this space, Kubernetes,
Docker containers

00:03:55.450 --> 00:03:57.400
are on everyone's lips, right?

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:58.900
And that's been
the case, honestly,

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:00.920
for the past several years.

00:04:00.920 --> 00:04:04.420
But the thing that's changed
more recently is many

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:08.230
workflows, core workflows, and
core use cases are now being

00:04:08.230 --> 00:04:11.110
targeted for containerization
that previously may not have

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:13.450
been considered to be
a containerizable--

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:14.790
if I can make up that word--

00:04:14.790 --> 00:04:16.450
type of a workflow, right?

00:04:16.450 --> 00:04:18.910
Containers and
Kubernetes are no longer

00:04:18.910 --> 00:04:21.579
the exclusive domain of
stateless applications

00:04:21.579 --> 00:04:23.530
and freshly written
microservices.

00:04:23.530 --> 00:04:25.630
People want to use them
for much, much more.

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:27.254
And Kubernetes
orchestration isn't just

00:04:27.254 --> 00:04:29.350
making these expanded
use cases possible,

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:31.827
it's really making them easy.

00:04:31.827 --> 00:04:33.910
And when you think about
these expanded use cases,

00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:35.980
data is a big part
of them, right?

00:04:35.980 --> 00:04:38.740
We all know that Kubernetes
and containerization

00:04:38.740 --> 00:04:41.650
offers massive benefits for
packaging and mobilizing

00:04:41.650 --> 00:04:42.900
applications.

00:04:42.900 --> 00:04:46.360
And to extend those benefits
to core workflows that

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:49.679
involve data, you need a
persistent data management

00:04:49.679 --> 00:04:50.220
layer, right?

00:04:50.220 --> 00:04:52.570
You need something to be
able to store and manage data

00:04:52.570 --> 00:04:55.360
beyond the lifecycle of a
given container or a given pod.

00:04:55.360 --> 00:04:57.130
So these types of
solutions become

00:04:57.130 --> 00:05:00.820
necessary as you extend the
scope of workflows and use

00:05:00.820 --> 00:05:04.287
cases that you're
leveraging with containers.

00:05:04.287 --> 00:05:06.870
Lots of different applications
have these requirements, right,

00:05:06.870 --> 00:05:08.770
from examples that
involve web services,

00:05:08.770 --> 00:05:11.460
like we'll discuss today,
to any kind of example that

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:14.160
persists or manages user
data, to a broad range

00:05:14.160 --> 00:05:16.560
of industry-specific
applications and domains,

00:05:16.560 --> 00:05:19.410
like scientific computing
for life sciences,

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:21.780
or financial services,
or media-entertainment.

00:05:21.780 --> 00:05:24.090
And there's many,
many more examples.

00:05:24.090 --> 00:05:26.850
Now fortunately,
Kubernetes, in anticipation

00:05:26.850 --> 00:05:29.220
of these requirements,
has evolved

00:05:29.220 --> 00:05:31.830
to supply services
that help you address

00:05:31.830 --> 00:05:35.190
these data-centric requirements
and data-centric needs, right?

00:05:35.190 --> 00:05:36.990
With Kubernetes
persistent volumes,

00:05:36.990 --> 00:05:39.405
you can seamlessly
integrate data storage,

00:05:39.405 --> 00:05:42.960
a persistent storage layer into
your Kubernetes deployments.

00:05:42.960 --> 00:05:45.090
With Kubernetes
storage classes, you

00:05:45.090 --> 00:05:49.050
can control and manage the
dynamic provisioning of storage

00:05:49.050 --> 00:05:51.486
by leveraging Kubernetes
volume provisioners.

00:05:51.486 --> 00:05:53.610
And with stateful sets,
you can manage the ordering

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:56.790
and management of certain
sets of pods or containers

00:05:56.790 --> 00:05:57.930
within Kubernetes.

00:05:57.930 --> 00:06:00.200
So with all of these
flexible capabilities

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:02.450
and integration options that
Kubernetes has given you,

00:06:02.450 --> 00:06:04.560
you have a lot of
choices and options

00:06:04.560 --> 00:06:05.970
for what to do on the data side.

00:06:05.970 --> 00:06:08.010
So what should I deploy as
a data management layer?

00:06:08.010 --> 00:06:08.760
What should it look like?

00:06:08.760 --> 00:06:10.490
Should I be deploying
file storage?

00:06:10.490 --> 00:06:11.635
Should I be targeting
block storage?

00:06:11.635 --> 00:06:13.200
Should I be looking at
open-source solutions?

00:06:13.200 --> 00:06:14.880
Should I be looking
at vended solutions?

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:17.040
So next, I'm going to go through
some of the key considerations,

00:06:17.040 --> 00:06:18.570
at least in our
view, that we think

00:06:18.570 --> 00:06:21.152
you should be taking into
account as you think about what

00:06:21.152 --> 00:06:23.610
types of stateful services and
what kind of data management

00:06:23.610 --> 00:06:25.500
layers and data
management platforms

00:06:25.500 --> 00:06:26.580
you should be deploying.

00:06:26.580 --> 00:06:28.440
And then I'll introduce you
to a qualifying solution

00:06:28.440 --> 00:06:30.630
from Elastifile and taking
you through the demo flow

00:06:30.630 --> 00:06:33.340
of our web services example.

00:06:33.340 --> 00:06:36.090
So first key consideration--
and there's going to be four--

00:06:36.090 --> 00:06:38.400
the first one that I want
to talk about is usability.

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:40.230
And when we think
about usability,

00:06:40.230 --> 00:06:43.050
we're really talking
about ease of integration,

00:06:43.050 --> 00:06:44.886
ease of adoption, ease of use.

00:06:44.886 --> 00:06:47.260
And from the data management
and the storage perspective,

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:50.070
you're really talking about how
you interface with the data.

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:52.349
And so really, what kind
of storage are you using?

00:06:52.349 --> 00:06:54.390
Are you using file storage,
using object storage,

00:06:54.390 --> 00:06:55.710
using block storage?

00:06:55.710 --> 00:06:58.860
And in our view, file
storage makes a lot of sense

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:01.500
as the default storage
platform and storage

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:04.200
interface for
stateful containers,

00:07:04.200 --> 00:07:07.380
for persistent storage
implemented with Kubernetes,

00:07:07.380 --> 00:07:10.020
because of the broad range
of applications and workflows

00:07:10.020 --> 00:07:12.870
today that rely on a file
system and expect a file

00:07:12.870 --> 00:07:14.080
system, right?

00:07:14.080 --> 00:07:16.470
At last year's Next, in
one of the presentations,

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:20.340
Descartes Labs noted
that 90% of the calls

00:07:20.340 --> 00:07:22.680
in the open-source GitHub
repository, the storage

00:07:22.680 --> 00:07:25.050
interface calls, were
calling fopen, right?

00:07:25.050 --> 00:07:27.330
So they were expecting a
POSIX-compliant file system

00:07:27.330 --> 00:07:28.090
underneath.

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:31.320
And those was, by far, the
most prevalent type of storage

00:07:31.320 --> 00:07:33.840
that was being called, more
than an order of magnitude

00:07:33.840 --> 00:07:36.180
over any other type of
storage, including object.

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:37.955
So really, I think
file is the way to go.

00:07:37.955 --> 00:07:40.080
In addition, of course, to
being application usable

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:42.450
and application
accessible, file systems

00:07:42.450 --> 00:07:45.009
are also human usable
and human accessible.

00:07:45.009 --> 00:07:46.800
You know, we're all
very familiar with file

00:07:46.800 --> 00:07:49.020
and directory hierarchies,
what those mean,

00:07:49.020 --> 00:07:50.250
how to traverse them.

00:07:50.250 --> 00:07:52.830
So leveraging a file system
provides a high level

00:07:52.830 --> 00:07:57.169
of usability in conjunction with
your containerized workflows.

00:07:57.169 --> 00:07:59.460
Another key criteria to
consider is scalability, right?

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:02.910
As we all know, Kubernetes
offers amazing capabilities

00:08:02.910 --> 00:08:06.390
for scaling application
deployments and scaling

00:08:06.390 --> 00:08:08.069
your ability to
handle workloads.

00:08:08.069 --> 00:08:10.360
So when you're implementing
a persistent storage layer,

00:08:10.360 --> 00:08:11.670
you want to complement that.

00:08:11.670 --> 00:08:13.128
You want to have
something that can

00:08:13.128 --> 00:08:15.360
scale to support the
capacity and performance

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:18.060
requirements at
the storage level

00:08:18.060 --> 00:08:20.250
to meet your needs of
today for your workflows,

00:08:20.250 --> 00:08:23.522
as well as you're evolving these
as your workflows may scale.

00:08:23.522 --> 00:08:25.759
Third key criteria is
shareability, right?

00:08:25.759 --> 00:08:28.050
You want to get away from
dealing with storage islands,

00:08:28.050 --> 00:08:28.250
right?

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:28.875
It's a problem.

00:08:28.875 --> 00:08:31.690
It can create a lot of
complexity in data management.

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:34.110
So if you have a solution that
can allow you to share data

00:08:34.110 --> 00:08:36.750
across your infrastructure--
so across your nodes,

00:08:36.750 --> 00:08:38.480
across your storage devices--

00:08:38.480 --> 00:08:40.169
and present single
namespaces that

00:08:40.169 --> 00:08:43.350
can scale out, that provides
huge benefits to enable

00:08:43.350 --> 00:08:46.620
seamless collaboration across
applications, across users,

00:08:46.620 --> 00:08:48.870
and across workflows.

00:08:48.870 --> 00:08:50.970
And the final key criteria
that I want to discuss

00:08:50.970 --> 00:08:52.980
is around data mobility, right?

00:08:52.980 --> 00:08:57.000
So as we all know, Kubernetes
provides extreme power

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:00.420
for application mobility, the
ability to package and mobilize

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:01.440
applications.

00:09:01.440 --> 00:09:03.660
So having complementary
capability

00:09:03.660 --> 00:09:05.880
to package and
mobilize your data

00:09:05.880 --> 00:09:07.620
can be hugely beneficial,
especially when

00:09:07.620 --> 00:09:10.710
we're talking about hybrid
workflows and the needs that

00:09:10.710 --> 00:09:13.470
arise to take data
from on-premises

00:09:13.470 --> 00:09:17.500
and be able to leverage and
access that data in the cloud.

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:19.010
So here are our
four key criteria.

00:09:19.010 --> 00:09:21.030
And now I'm going to talk a
little bit about the Elastifile

00:09:21.030 --> 00:09:23.280
solution-- and of course,
we feel that we meet these

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:24.660
criteria fairly well--

00:09:24.660 --> 00:09:27.690
and give you a sense for
how you can leverage us

00:09:27.690 --> 00:09:30.810
as we go through the demo flow.

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:34.110
So in a nutshell, the way
to think about Elastifile

00:09:34.110 --> 00:09:37.830
is enterprise-grade, scalable,
POSIX-compliant file storage

00:09:37.830 --> 00:09:39.240
for the cloud.

00:09:39.240 --> 00:09:41.820
You can deploy us
in GCP Marketplace.

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:45.130
And essentially, we consist
of two key components.

00:09:45.130 --> 00:09:47.550
One is the core file system
itself, the Elastifile Cloud

00:09:47.550 --> 00:09:48.480
File System.

00:09:48.480 --> 00:09:50.270
Or ECFS is what we
call it for short.

00:09:50.270 --> 00:09:52.770
It's a software-defined,
scale-out, distributed file

00:09:52.770 --> 00:09:53.970
system.

00:09:53.970 --> 00:09:56.070
The other key component
of the Elastifile solution

00:09:56.070 --> 00:09:58.410
is what we call
Elastifile CloudConnect.

00:09:58.410 --> 00:10:00.660
And what CloudConnect
does is it delivers

00:10:00.660 --> 00:10:04.050
data mobility between file
systems and object storage.

00:10:04.050 --> 00:10:06.930
And it really allows you to
combine those two storage

00:10:06.930 --> 00:10:09.332
paradigms to get the
best of both worlds.

00:10:09.332 --> 00:10:11.040
And I'll go into a
little bit more detail

00:10:11.040 --> 00:10:12.623
about how CloudConnect
comes into play

00:10:12.623 --> 00:10:14.360
in these sorts of
containerized workflows

00:10:14.360 --> 00:10:17.710
and across hybrid
clouds in just a moment.

00:10:17.710 --> 00:10:19.440
But first, a bit about
the file system--

00:10:19.440 --> 00:10:23.100
so ECFS, it's a software-only,
environment-agnostic file

00:10:23.100 --> 00:10:23.820
system, right?

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:25.230
So you can run it in any cloud.

00:10:25.230 --> 00:10:27.030
You can run it
on-premises as well.

00:10:27.030 --> 00:10:29.250
And essentially, what it's
doing behind the scenes,

00:10:29.250 --> 00:10:33.450
it's aggregating the storage
resources of your cloud VMs

00:10:33.450 --> 00:10:35.520
and presenting them
to your applications

00:10:35.520 --> 00:10:37.800
in a single shared namespace.

00:10:37.800 --> 00:10:39.760
And what it delivers
to those applications

00:10:39.760 --> 00:10:41.890
is POSIX-compliant
primary storage.

00:10:41.890 --> 00:10:43.500
And when I say
primary source, I mean

00:10:43.500 --> 00:10:45.810
this storage is meant for
applications, and workflows,

00:10:45.810 --> 00:10:47.643
and users to interface
with directly, right?

00:10:47.643 --> 00:10:49.680
It can support
transactional workloads.

00:10:49.680 --> 00:10:51.330
And it's really
built for the cloud.

00:10:51.330 --> 00:10:53.550
All of the services that
underlie the Elastifile

00:10:53.550 --> 00:10:55.620
solution are distributed,
including the metadata

00:10:55.620 --> 00:10:56.320
management.

00:10:56.320 --> 00:10:58.260
As many of you who deal
with storage may know,

00:10:58.260 --> 00:11:00.301
metadata management can
often become a bottleneck

00:11:00.301 --> 00:11:02.530
to performance as
storage systems scale.

00:11:02.530 --> 00:11:04.470
So our solution was
designed for the cloud.

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:06.424
And so the metadata
management model

00:11:06.424 --> 00:11:08.340
was implemented in a
fully distributed fashion

00:11:08.340 --> 00:11:11.040
to ensure that you
can scale effectively

00:11:11.040 --> 00:11:13.190
with performance scaling
linearly as you scale out.

00:11:16.290 --> 00:11:19.060
Now, to the user, what
Elastifile provides

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:21.320
is scalable capacity
and performance.

00:11:21.320 --> 00:11:22.890
You have the ability
to add capacity

00:11:22.890 --> 00:11:25.470
to your cluster in a hot
fashion and dynamic fashion

00:11:25.470 --> 00:11:26.960
without bringing
the cluster down.

00:11:26.960 --> 00:11:28.570
You can remove it if necessary.

00:11:28.570 --> 00:11:30.200
And as I mentioned
before, performance

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:32.760
is going to scale out linearly
as you add capacity, as well.

00:11:32.760 --> 00:11:34.470
Full enterprise
feature set, including

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:36.780
snapshots, data reduction
through compression,

00:11:36.780 --> 00:11:39.720
deduplication high
availability, asynchronous

00:11:39.720 --> 00:11:41.190
replication, et cetera.

00:11:41.190 --> 00:11:43.790
Everything that you can do
with Elastifile through our GUI

00:11:43.790 --> 00:11:46.890
and through our CLI is also
fully REST API controllable.

00:11:46.890 --> 00:11:49.440
So you can implement
seamless workflows leveraging

00:11:49.440 --> 00:11:52.440
infrastructure as code,
like Terraform or Ansible

00:11:52.440 --> 00:11:53.761
as well with Elastifile.

00:11:53.761 --> 00:11:56.010
And as I mentioned, it's
easily deployable at the push

00:11:56.010 --> 00:11:58.479
of a button through
the GCP Marketplace.

00:11:58.479 --> 00:12:00.520
So I'll just quickly show
you a quick deployment.

00:12:00.520 --> 00:12:02.640
We're going to spin up an
Elastifile cluster here.

00:12:02.640 --> 00:12:04.681
So we're going to call
this one Google Next Demo.

00:12:04.681 --> 00:12:06.589
So you can do this
through Marketplace today.

00:12:06.589 --> 00:12:08.880
You're going to select your
zone, some basic networking

00:12:08.880 --> 00:12:11.190
information, and you're
going to hit Deploy.

00:12:11.190 --> 00:12:13.380
Now what's actually
going to deploy here

00:12:13.380 --> 00:12:15.630
is the Elastifile management VM.

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:18.720
That management VM is going
to give you access to our UI.

00:12:18.720 --> 00:12:20.550
And from within our
UI, you're going

00:12:20.550 --> 00:12:22.980
to be able to specify the
characteristics of the cluster,

00:12:22.980 --> 00:12:25.188
the storage cluster that
you want to create, the most

00:12:25.188 --> 00:12:27.030
important one, of course,
being the capacity

00:12:27.030 --> 00:12:28.570
that you want to create.

00:12:28.570 --> 00:12:30.720
So here we're logging
into the Elastifile UI.

00:12:30.720 --> 00:12:32.678
So when that's done, you
can see our management

00:12:32.678 --> 00:12:34.999
VM is up and running from
our management console there.

00:12:34.999 --> 00:12:37.290
We're going to select the
type of storage that we want,

00:12:37.290 --> 00:12:39.300
and we're going to add
9 terabytes of capacity

00:12:39.300 --> 00:12:40.870
for this particular cluster.

00:12:40.870 --> 00:12:43.590
In this case, that's going
to correspond to spinning up

00:12:43.590 --> 00:12:45.905
three instances in GCP.

00:12:45.905 --> 00:12:48.280
And if we go to the Compute
Engine, we'll be able to see.

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:50.822
You can see the management VM,
as well as those three storage

00:12:50.822 --> 00:12:52.779
nodes that are going to
be part of our cluster.

00:12:52.779 --> 00:12:54.130
They've all been started.

00:12:54.130 --> 00:12:56.400
And so if we go back
to the Elastifile UI,

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:58.500
you'll be able to see
the deployment finishes.

00:12:58.500 --> 00:13:01.530
And now you have a cluster
available with the capacity

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:02.850
that you specified.

00:13:02.850 --> 00:13:04.620
So that's how easy it is, right?

00:13:04.620 --> 00:13:07.244
You can just spin it up at
the click of a button, GCP,

00:13:07.244 --> 00:13:09.410
put the capacity you want,
and we're up and running.

00:13:09.410 --> 00:13:11.640
Now through that UI that you
just saw a preview of there,

00:13:11.640 --> 00:13:13.080
you can do lots of other things.

00:13:13.080 --> 00:13:15.390
You can manage capacity
and monitor capacity.

00:13:15.390 --> 00:13:17.440
You can also manage and
monitor performance.

00:13:17.440 --> 00:13:18.930
You can also look at the
status in your environment,

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:20.850
like the number of
nodes in your cluster,

00:13:20.850 --> 00:13:23.266
the number of physical devices,
the number of connections,

00:13:23.266 --> 00:13:24.420
et cetera.

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:26.900
You can also scale the cluster
through that same UI, right?

00:13:26.900 --> 00:13:29.066
You saw us deploy it and
specify a certain capacity.

00:13:29.066 --> 00:13:30.210
You can also scale it.

00:13:30.210 --> 00:13:33.499
And we're continually
offering more and more options

00:13:33.499 --> 00:13:35.790
for the types of storage and
the granularity of storage

00:13:35.790 --> 00:13:38.940
that you can deploy in a
cluster to give you more options

00:13:38.940 --> 00:13:41.610
to create the types of cost
and performance trade-offs

00:13:41.610 --> 00:13:43.360
that you want in your
environments, right?

00:13:43.360 --> 00:13:45.930
Even since the time when we
recorded that previous demo

00:13:45.930 --> 00:13:48.540
clip, we've added
additional options

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:50.760
for adding capacity and
deployment capacity.

00:13:50.760 --> 00:13:53.010
You can see here, we're
showing SSD persistent disks

00:13:53.010 --> 00:13:55.662
and standard persistent disks.

00:13:55.662 --> 00:13:57.370
You can also drill
down into performance.

00:13:57.370 --> 00:13:58.911
You can look at it
at the high level.

00:13:58.911 --> 00:14:01.000
You can also drill down
into certain time scales.

00:14:01.000 --> 00:14:04.060
You can also look at
performance for certain--

00:14:04.060 --> 00:14:06.460
call them collections of
data that we call Elastifile

00:14:06.460 --> 00:14:08.530
file data containers
that are segments

00:14:08.530 --> 00:14:11.270
of data within your file
system that you can choose

00:14:11.270 --> 00:14:13.030
at your will, as desired.

00:14:13.030 --> 00:14:14.710
And a data container
is something

00:14:14.710 --> 00:14:17.410
that you can basically
configure with certain policies.

00:14:17.410 --> 00:14:19.090
So you can say, for
this data container,

00:14:19.090 --> 00:14:20.860
this collection of
files and directories,

00:14:20.860 --> 00:14:23.230
I want compression to be on, or
I want deduplication to be on,

00:14:23.230 --> 00:14:24.510
or I want both of them to be on.

00:14:24.510 --> 00:14:25.676
And you can also set quotas.

00:14:25.676 --> 00:14:28.150
So a way of managing
collections of data inside

00:14:28.150 --> 00:14:30.469
of your contiguous file system.

00:14:30.469 --> 00:14:32.260
And finally, you also
have this system view

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:34.509
that enables you to see the
health of your environment

00:14:34.509 --> 00:14:36.960
on a continuous basis.

00:14:36.960 --> 00:14:39.600
Now once you've got an
Elastifile file system spun up

00:14:39.600 --> 00:14:41.850
in the cloud, you want to
leverage it with Kubernetes,

00:14:41.850 --> 00:14:42.349
right?

00:14:42.349 --> 00:14:43.990
So to connect it
into Kubernetes,

00:14:43.990 --> 00:14:46.670
we've also just
released, just last week,

00:14:46.670 --> 00:14:48.190
the Elastifile
storage provisioner,

00:14:48.190 --> 00:14:50.057
also available through
GCP Marketplace.

00:14:50.057 --> 00:14:51.640
And what the storage
provisioner does,

00:14:51.640 --> 00:14:53.723
kind of calling back to
those Kubernetes functions

00:14:53.723 --> 00:14:56.830
that I mentioned before,
it creates a storage class

00:14:56.830 --> 00:14:59.290
for Elastifile
storage within GCP.

00:14:59.290 --> 00:15:01.900
And through that storage
class, which you can see here,

00:15:01.900 --> 00:15:03.440
you can connect a cluster.

00:15:03.440 --> 00:15:05.920
And you can now specify
persistent volume claims

00:15:05.920 --> 00:15:09.370
and persistent volumes that
leverage and deploy Elastifile

00:15:09.370 --> 00:15:11.840
storage on a dynamic basis.

00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:14.580
So now you have the ability to
connect in Elastifile simply

00:15:14.580 --> 00:15:18.224
into any Kubernetes deployment
by leveraging this provisioner.

00:15:18.224 --> 00:15:20.140
So if we go back to our
checklist really quick

00:15:20.140 --> 00:15:21.250
and see how we're doing--

00:15:21.250 --> 00:15:23.770
usability, file
interface, right?

00:15:23.770 --> 00:15:25.900
We provide NFS file
storage, so we're

00:15:25.900 --> 00:15:27.910
very user and
application-compatible.

00:15:27.910 --> 00:15:29.320
It's POSIX-compliant.

00:15:29.320 --> 00:15:33.050
And the key thing around POSIX
is the strict consistency.

00:15:33.050 --> 00:15:36.520
So if an application writes
data to our shared namespace,

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:38.655
and another application
tries to read data,

00:15:38.655 --> 00:15:40.030
the application
that's reading is

00:15:40.030 --> 00:15:42.880
going to get the latest data
that was written, right?

00:15:42.880 --> 00:15:44.230
It's strictly consistent.

00:15:44.230 --> 00:15:46.339
Fully REST API-controlled,
as I mentioned before.

00:15:46.339 --> 00:15:48.130
And we have a Kubernetes
volume provisioner

00:15:48.130 --> 00:15:48.985
available in the Marketplace.

00:15:48.985 --> 00:15:50.920
So I think we're
doing OK on usability.

00:15:50.920 --> 00:15:52.345
Scalability, as I
mentioned, fully

00:15:52.345 --> 00:15:53.470
distributed metadata model.

00:15:53.470 --> 00:15:55.690
So it allows you to scale
out with linear performance

00:15:55.690 --> 00:15:56.189
scaling.

00:15:56.189 --> 00:15:58.840
You can add capacity, remove
capacity as necessary--

00:15:58.840 --> 00:16:00.400
so highly elastic.

00:16:00.400 --> 00:16:02.620
Shareability-- again,
it's a shared file system,

00:16:02.620 --> 00:16:04.220
so you're unifying
all these resources

00:16:04.220 --> 00:16:05.750
within a single namespace.

00:16:05.750 --> 00:16:09.342
So all VMs, all pods, all
users can access the same data

00:16:09.342 --> 00:16:11.050
regardless of where
they're connecting in

00:16:11.050 --> 00:16:12.159
from the file space.

00:16:12.159 --> 00:16:14.200
So the one piece that we
haven't talked about yet

00:16:14.200 --> 00:16:15.760
is the data mobility piece.

00:16:15.760 --> 00:16:18.094
And that's where
CloudConnect comes into play.

00:16:18.094 --> 00:16:20.260
So as I mentioned, CloudConnect
manages the transfer

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:21.890
between file and object.

00:16:21.890 --> 00:16:24.160
And it's a bidirectional
transfer that it can manage.

00:16:24.160 --> 00:16:27.400
And it really blends the best
of both worlds in use cases

00:16:27.400 --> 00:16:30.880
that involve both hybrid data
management-- so from on-prem

00:16:30.880 --> 00:16:32.800
to cloud-- and also
in-cloud data management

00:16:32.800 --> 00:16:34.330
between object and file.

00:16:34.330 --> 00:16:37.390
So the thing to understand
about CloudConnect

00:16:37.390 --> 00:16:41.920
is, really, what it's doing
is it's helping you to encode

00:16:41.920 --> 00:16:45.260
the data of any given file
system into object storage

00:16:45.260 --> 00:16:47.442
so that it can be stored
there in an inactive state

00:16:47.442 --> 00:16:48.650
until you're ready to use it.

00:16:48.650 --> 00:16:50.110
And then when you're
ready to use it,

00:16:50.110 --> 00:16:52.276
you can pull the data out
again, using CloudConnect,

00:16:52.276 --> 00:16:53.690
into another file system.

00:16:53.690 --> 00:16:56.510
So in a hybrid mode, like
what I'm highlighting here,

00:16:56.510 --> 00:16:58.700
what we would do is you can
start with data on-prem,

00:16:58.700 --> 00:17:00.190
from any on-premesis
file system.

00:17:00.190 --> 00:17:02.760
It doesn't have to be
Elastifile, but it could be.

00:17:02.760 --> 00:17:05.640
What CloudConnect will do is
it will compress the data.

00:17:05.640 --> 00:17:06.880
It will dedup the data.

00:17:06.880 --> 00:17:09.890
It will encode the data
into object storage format--

00:17:09.890 --> 00:17:12.604
so in this case,
into a GCS bucket.

00:17:12.604 --> 00:17:14.770
And we're going to retain
all the file and directory

00:17:14.770 --> 00:17:17.386
hierarchy information, all
the attributes, all the links,

00:17:17.386 --> 00:17:18.760
everything about
the file system,

00:17:18.760 --> 00:17:20.829
but encoded into object format.

00:17:20.829 --> 00:17:23.290
Then when you're ready to
use that data in the cloud

00:17:23.290 --> 00:17:25.839
and processes it natively
in your file system

00:17:25.839 --> 00:17:27.760
in your primary
storage, CloudConnect

00:17:27.760 --> 00:17:30.180
can also allow you to
then check out the data.

00:17:30.180 --> 00:17:32.740
So putting it from file
to object is the check-in.

00:17:32.740 --> 00:17:34.660
You can also check out
the data from object

00:17:34.660 --> 00:17:37.436
to file and put it into
an Elastifile file system

00:17:37.436 --> 00:17:38.810
that you're running
in the cloud.

00:17:38.810 --> 00:17:40.210
You don't have to pull
out the whole namespace.

00:17:40.210 --> 00:17:42.160
You can pull out certain
files, directories.

00:17:42.160 --> 00:17:43.902
Up to you what you
want to pull out.

00:17:43.902 --> 00:17:46.360
So again, we try to be very
efficient about how we transfer

00:17:46.360 --> 00:17:49.210
the data, because that
can be a painful component

00:17:49.210 --> 00:17:50.900
for many organizations.

00:17:50.900 --> 00:17:52.090
So we compress the data.

00:17:52.090 --> 00:17:52.971
We dedup the data.

00:17:52.971 --> 00:17:54.970
And after an initial
synchronization, let's say,

00:17:54.970 --> 00:17:56.860
from file to object
in the cloud,

00:17:56.860 --> 00:17:59.609
any additional transfers will
only ship across the diffs.

00:17:59.609 --> 00:18:01.150
So we try to be very
efficient there.

00:18:01.150 --> 00:18:03.520
And CloudConnect is also
fully REST API-controllable.

00:18:03.520 --> 00:18:05.860
So anything you can
do through our UIs,

00:18:05.860 --> 00:18:08.590
you can do it
using REST as well.

00:18:08.590 --> 00:18:10.720
And the key thing to think
about with CloudConnect

00:18:10.720 --> 00:18:12.442
is this concept of a connection.

00:18:12.442 --> 00:18:13.900
And this connection,
the connection

00:18:13.900 --> 00:18:16.270
has a source and a target.

00:18:16.270 --> 00:18:18.939
And in the case of going
from on-prem to the cloud,

00:18:18.939 --> 00:18:20.980
your on-prem location is
going to be your source,

00:18:20.980 --> 00:18:23.769
and your cloud bucket is
going to be your target.

00:18:23.769 --> 00:18:25.810
So now, let's go back to
our use case example, so

00:18:25.810 --> 00:18:26.859
our ElastiFizz.

00:18:26.859 --> 00:18:28.150
We've got this amazing product.

00:18:28.150 --> 00:18:29.514
We've got this commercial.

00:18:29.514 --> 00:18:31.180
We're ready to scale
our infrastructure.

00:18:31.180 --> 00:18:32.800
So as a starting
point, we've got

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:35.380
some new data for our
ElastiFizz campaign

00:18:35.380 --> 00:18:36.580
that we've got on-premises.

00:18:36.580 --> 00:18:38.680
And we want to get it
into the cloud, right?

00:18:38.680 --> 00:18:41.037
To start off, we have a little
bit of data in the cloud,

00:18:41.037 --> 00:18:41.870
as you can see here.

00:18:41.870 --> 00:18:44.441
We've got a couple simple
Elastifile logos and stuff,

00:18:44.441 --> 00:18:46.690
but we want to bring over
some of these new characters

00:18:46.690 --> 00:18:49.720
and some additional logos to
get ready for this commercial

00:18:49.720 --> 00:18:52.510
and the influx of visitors that
we're expecting to our website.

00:18:52.510 --> 00:18:55.250
So we want to stage this data
and get it into the cloud.

00:18:55.250 --> 00:18:56.940
So we're going to
use CloudConnect

00:18:56.940 --> 00:18:59.610
to bring the data over from
our on-premises location

00:18:59.610 --> 00:19:01.972
into GCP and into GCS.

00:19:01.972 --> 00:19:03.680
And that's what we're
going to show here.

00:19:03.680 --> 00:19:05.074
And it's a pretty
simple process.

00:19:05.074 --> 00:19:06.490
Now the first thing
is, let's just

00:19:06.490 --> 00:19:07.940
look at the state
of our environment.

00:19:07.940 --> 00:19:09.980
So we go to our WordPress back
end and our media library.

00:19:09.980 --> 00:19:12.284
And you can see, as I
mentioned, we've got one logo.

00:19:12.284 --> 00:19:13.450
So it's pretty lonely there.

00:19:13.450 --> 00:19:14.200
It's kind of poor.

00:19:14.200 --> 00:19:15.900
So let's go into CloudConnect.

00:19:15.900 --> 00:19:17.310
So we go to the Elastifile UI.

00:19:17.310 --> 00:19:19.407
We're going to select
Google, of course.

00:19:19.407 --> 00:19:20.990
And we're going to
call this an upload

00:19:20.990 --> 00:19:24.410
connection, because we're taking
data from on-prem to the cloud.

00:19:24.410 --> 00:19:26.677
Our bucket name is
going to be gcp-demo-1.

00:19:26.677 --> 00:19:29.010
And in this case, if we go
to the Google Cloud back end,

00:19:29.010 --> 00:19:32.080
just to show you, gcp-demo-1
doesn't currently exist.

00:19:32.080 --> 00:19:34.320
That bucket has not
yet been created.

00:19:34.320 --> 00:19:37.440
We're going to create it as part
of this process, automatically.

00:19:37.440 --> 00:19:39.779
So we're going to select
a check-in connection,

00:19:39.779 --> 00:19:42.320
and we're going to go ahead and
perform a check-in connection

00:19:42.320 --> 00:19:44.210
creation operation here.

00:19:44.210 --> 00:19:46.280
Now what we're doing
now is we're going here,

00:19:46.280 --> 00:19:48.590
and we're going to grab
the source directory

00:19:48.590 --> 00:19:51.306
from our on-premisis
environment that has the data

00:19:51.306 --> 00:19:52.430
that we want to bring over.

00:19:52.430 --> 00:19:54.721
So these are our additional
logos and files, et cetera.

00:19:54.721 --> 00:19:56.430
So now we created
the connection.

00:19:56.430 --> 00:19:57.410
And we're going to
move forward, and we're

00:19:57.410 --> 00:19:59.034
going to actually
perform the check-in.

00:19:59.034 --> 00:20:01.430
And we'll give it a
description of "version 1."

00:20:01.430 --> 00:20:03.800
So when you go back
to the Google Cloud UI

00:20:03.800 --> 00:20:06.532
after the check-in has
been created and performed,

00:20:06.532 --> 00:20:07.490
we're going to refresh.

00:20:07.490 --> 00:20:10.027
And you'll be able to
see, gcp-demo-1 has now

00:20:10.027 --> 00:20:10.860
been created, right?

00:20:10.860 --> 00:20:12.891
This bucket that didn't
exist is now available.

00:20:12.891 --> 00:20:14.390
And if we click
into the bucket, you

00:20:14.390 --> 00:20:15.980
can see it's full of objects.

00:20:15.980 --> 00:20:18.650
These objects represent
the file system data

00:20:18.650 --> 00:20:21.609
that was encoded leveraging
CloudConnect-- again, retaining

00:20:21.609 --> 00:20:23.150
all the file and
directory hierarchy,

00:20:23.150 --> 00:20:25.590
all the links, all the
attributes of that file system,

00:20:25.590 --> 00:20:28.130
but stored in cheap and deep
object format, ready for you

00:20:28.130 --> 00:20:29.331
to use.

00:20:29.331 --> 00:20:30.830
So now we've got
our additional data

00:20:30.830 --> 00:20:34.260
for our campaign staged
in GCS in the cloud.

00:20:34.260 --> 00:20:36.320
So let's load it into the
Elastifile file system,

00:20:36.320 --> 00:20:38.570
because we are going to run
our web services infrastructure

00:20:38.570 --> 00:20:39.890
on top of Elastifile, right?

00:20:39.890 --> 00:20:41.810
Just like we were
running it on-prem,

00:20:41.810 --> 00:20:43.490
we're going to run
it using Kubernetes

00:20:43.490 --> 00:20:45.050
in the cloud on
top of Elastifile

00:20:45.050 --> 00:20:47.130
to extend our infrastructure.

00:20:47.130 --> 00:20:50.030
So let's do that.

00:20:50.030 --> 00:20:52.820
So you can see now, we're going
to create a download connection

00:20:52.820 --> 00:20:54.010
to check the data out.

00:20:54.010 --> 00:20:55.968
Again, we're going to
specify that same bucket,

00:20:55.968 --> 00:20:58.100
because we're pulling
the data from gcp-demo-1.

00:20:58.100 --> 00:21:02.030
And now our target is going to
actually be a file system that

00:21:02.030 --> 00:21:04.550
corresponds to a persistent
volume claim that

00:21:04.550 --> 00:21:07.349
was created using Elastifile's
storage provisioner, right?

00:21:07.349 --> 00:21:09.890
So we connected into Kubernetes
with that storage provisioner

00:21:09.890 --> 00:21:11.310
in our storage class.

00:21:11.310 --> 00:21:13.860
So we're going to specify that
is going to be our target.

00:21:13.860 --> 00:21:15.170
So we put that data in there.

00:21:15.170 --> 00:21:18.327
And we're going to create
our check-out connection.

00:21:18.327 --> 00:21:19.910
So with that connection
created, we're

00:21:19.910 --> 00:21:23.770
gonig to actually
perform the check-out.

00:21:23.770 --> 00:21:25.574
And here you can
see some options

00:21:25.574 --> 00:21:27.240
for how you handle
files in a check-out,

00:21:27.240 --> 00:21:29.170
if they were already
existing, et cetera.

00:21:29.170 --> 00:21:31.440
So we'll pull the check-out.

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:32.150
That happens.

00:21:32.150 --> 00:21:33.900
If we look in our
in-cloud file system,

00:21:33.900 --> 00:21:36.490
you can see an image upload
directory has been created.

00:21:36.490 --> 00:21:38.165
That wasn't there before.

00:21:38.165 --> 00:21:40.540
And if you look, there's
several files in that directory.

00:21:40.540 --> 00:21:42.050
So just to close
the story, we'll

00:21:42.050 --> 00:21:43.630
go back to our media library.

00:21:43.630 --> 00:21:44.130
We'll look.

00:21:44.130 --> 00:21:46.421
OK, image upload directory,
that's the one that we just

00:21:46.421 --> 00:21:47.850
created with our check-out

00:21:47.850 --> 00:21:49.560
We're going to
select a few files,

00:21:49.560 --> 00:21:51.510
pull them into our
media content library.

00:21:51.510 --> 00:21:54.689
We'll take a look and
see what they are.

00:21:54.689 --> 00:21:55.730
And there you can see it.

00:21:55.730 --> 00:21:57.310
We got some additional logos.

00:21:57.310 --> 00:21:59.150
We've got our cool
little characters.

00:21:59.150 --> 00:22:00.430
So now we're ready, right?

00:22:00.430 --> 00:22:03.250
Our website is now primed
with the additional content

00:22:03.250 --> 00:22:05.800
that we want to leverage in
our scale-out infrastructure

00:22:05.800 --> 00:22:08.644
to support the influx of
visitors from our commercial.

00:22:08.644 --> 00:22:09.310
So that's great.

00:22:09.310 --> 00:22:10.890
So we've got the data
in the file system.

00:22:10.890 --> 00:22:11.390
Yep.

00:22:11.390 --> 00:22:12.041
Question?

00:22:12.041 --> 00:22:13.454
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]?

00:22:17.700 --> 00:22:20.670
JEROME MCFARLAND: So you can
use rsync to bring data across.

00:22:20.670 --> 00:22:24.150
But with CloudConnect, you get
a lot of granular capabilities

00:22:24.150 --> 00:22:26.994
to manage the lifecycle of
the data in that you can have

00:22:26.994 --> 00:22:29.410
the data stored in an object
until you're ready to use it,

00:22:29.410 --> 00:22:29.630
let's say.

00:22:29.630 --> 00:22:31.380
And you can pull it out
into the file system.

00:22:31.380 --> 00:22:32.970
When you're done with the data
and processing with the file

00:22:32.970 --> 00:22:34.750
system, you can push
it back into object

00:22:34.750 --> 00:22:36.000
and spin down the file system.

00:22:36.000 --> 00:22:38.754
So CloudConnect gives you a
little bit more granularity

00:22:38.754 --> 00:22:40.420
on how you manage the
lifecycle of data,

00:22:40.420 --> 00:22:43.420
whether it's stored in
object or in file, right?

00:22:43.420 --> 00:22:46.130
So again, we're trying to blend
the best of both worlds there.

00:22:46.130 --> 00:22:48.810
rsync might be characterized
as one of those manual methods

00:22:48.810 --> 00:22:50.727
to bring the data over
that I mentioned before

00:22:50.727 --> 00:22:53.060
All right, so now we've got
the data in our file system.

00:22:53.060 --> 00:22:53.940
So we're ready to go.

00:22:53.940 --> 00:22:55.789
So let's just do a
little bit of work.

00:22:55.789 --> 00:22:57.830
Before we do the work,
actually, let me come back

00:22:57.830 --> 00:22:59.100
to the data mobility
piece and just

00:22:59.100 --> 00:23:00.880
make sure we talk
about-- we close that.

00:23:00.880 --> 00:23:02.850
So we've shown hybrid
cloud connectivity.

00:23:02.850 --> 00:23:04.860
We've brought data from
on-prem into the cloud.

00:23:04.860 --> 00:23:07.650
And we've shown the connectivity
between file and object.

00:23:07.650 --> 00:23:10.000
And one thing I didn't
mention is that CloudConnect

00:23:10.000 --> 00:23:11.550
is bidirectional, of course.

00:23:11.550 --> 00:23:14.015
You know, I showed a flow
bringing data from on-prem

00:23:14.015 --> 00:23:15.390
into object, and
then from object

00:23:15.390 --> 00:23:17.020
into the file
system in the cloud.

00:23:17.020 --> 00:23:18.690
You can also bring
data back, right?

00:23:18.690 --> 00:23:21.150
You can bring it back into
object from the file system.

00:23:21.150 --> 00:23:23.580
You can take it from object
back into your on-premisis

00:23:23.580 --> 00:23:25.099
environment, if so desired.

00:23:25.099 --> 00:23:26.890
So I think we've covered
the data mobility.

00:23:26.890 --> 00:23:28.120
So let's just do a
little bit of work.

00:23:28.120 --> 00:23:29.820
Let's make sure that everything
kind of fits together

00:23:29.820 --> 00:23:31.292
and actually works properly.

00:23:31.292 --> 00:23:33.750
So here, we've already got a
WordPress deployment spun out.

00:23:33.750 --> 00:23:35.300
You can see one WordPress pod.

00:23:35.300 --> 00:23:36.758
I also want to
highlight that we've

00:23:36.758 --> 00:23:38.436
got this db loader pod here.

00:23:38.436 --> 00:23:39.810
And what that's
doing is actually

00:23:39.810 --> 00:23:42.390
generating synthetic
I/O reads and writes

00:23:42.390 --> 00:23:44.380
on the back end of our
storage infrastructure

00:23:44.380 --> 00:23:45.870
and just hitting the
Elastifile cluster

00:23:45.870 --> 00:23:46.994
with some reads and writes.

00:23:46.994 --> 00:23:49.320
So we want to make the
cluster work in addition

00:23:49.320 --> 00:23:50.940
to the read commands
that are going

00:23:50.940 --> 00:23:53.340
to be coming in through JMeter,
which is what we're spinning up

00:23:53.340 --> 00:23:55.860
here, generating some workload
through our web infrastructure.

00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:57.735
So we've got the web
workload, and we've also

00:23:57.735 --> 00:24:00.334
got some back-end I/O. And
that will come into play later.

00:24:00.334 --> 00:24:02.250
So you can see everything
seems to be working.

00:24:02.250 --> 00:24:04.794
We're handling about 44,000
read requests per minute.

00:24:04.794 --> 00:24:06.960
So we're able to handle
some traffic to our website.

00:24:06.960 --> 00:24:07.920
So that's interesting.

00:24:07.920 --> 00:24:10.050
But really, with Kubernetes,
what you want to be able to do

00:24:10.050 --> 00:24:11.610
is leverage the
scalability, right?

00:24:11.610 --> 00:24:13.280
So let's scale the
front end, right?

00:24:13.280 --> 00:24:15.610
Let's get some additional
WordPress front-end pods

00:24:15.610 --> 00:24:18.230
going here in our
GKE deployment.

00:24:18.230 --> 00:24:20.020
So that's what we're
going to do next.

00:24:20.020 --> 00:24:22.380
So here you can see, got
the initial WordPress pod

00:24:22.380 --> 00:24:23.100
that's there.

00:24:23.100 --> 00:24:26.530
So we're going to scale to
four replicas using kubectl.

00:24:26.530 --> 00:24:27.510
We'll do that.

00:24:27.510 --> 00:24:29.820
You'll be able to see
there's now four WordPress

00:24:29.820 --> 00:24:31.390
replicas up and running.

00:24:31.390 --> 00:24:32.700
So we scaled our front end.

00:24:32.700 --> 00:24:35.075
And you're going to see the
performance is going to scale

00:24:35.075 --> 00:24:36.760
up of our cluster, as well.

00:24:36.760 --> 00:24:38.760
So we went from
around 45, and we're

00:24:38.760 --> 00:24:41.880
going to scale up to about
70,000 requests handled

00:24:41.880 --> 00:24:43.390
per minute.

00:24:43.390 --> 00:24:46.040
Now of course,
scaling the front end

00:24:46.040 --> 00:24:47.851
is only one piece of the story.

00:24:47.851 --> 00:24:50.100
And Kubernetes provide
seamless capability to do that,

00:24:50.100 --> 00:24:51.330
as we just discussed.

00:24:51.330 --> 00:24:53.469
Scaling the back end
and scaling the storage

00:24:53.469 --> 00:24:55.260
can also be an important
part of the story.

00:24:55.260 --> 00:24:56.801
What if you need
additional capacity?

00:24:56.801 --> 00:24:59.460
What if storage has become the
bottleneck to the performance

00:24:59.460 --> 00:25:00.585
for your given application?

00:25:00.585 --> 00:25:02.850
So you need more
scale-our performance.

00:25:02.850 --> 00:25:05.410
So of course, with Elastifile,
as I mentioned previously,

00:25:05.410 --> 00:25:06.995
you can scale the
file system as well.

00:25:06.995 --> 00:25:09.120
So we're going to show you,
quickly, how to do that

00:25:09.120 --> 00:25:11.730
as well to control all parts
of your infrastructure.

00:25:11.730 --> 00:25:13.920
So as I mentioned before,
we had that one db loader

00:25:13.920 --> 00:25:16.003
that was running and
generating some synthetic I/O

00:25:16.003 --> 00:25:16.710
on the back end.

00:25:16.710 --> 00:25:18.700
So before we scale
our cluster, we're

00:25:18.700 --> 00:25:19.950
going to scale that db loader.

00:25:19.950 --> 00:25:22.420
So you can see we've now created
four replicas for that db

00:25:22.420 --> 00:25:22.920
loader.

00:25:22.920 --> 00:25:25.380
So we're increasing
the amount of I/O

00:25:25.380 --> 00:25:26.980
that's hitting our file system.

00:25:26.980 --> 00:25:29.105
And the reason that we do
that is because we wanted

00:25:29.105 --> 00:25:31.740
to be able to show, in the
performance UI for Elastifile,

00:25:31.740 --> 00:25:35.044
you can see the performance of
the cluster scaling up, right?

00:25:35.044 --> 00:25:36.960
In conjunction with that
additional I/O that's

00:25:36.960 --> 00:25:39.251
being hit, we can see the
additional performance growth

00:25:39.251 --> 00:25:41.430
there in the UI, all
while actually maintaining

00:25:41.430 --> 00:25:44.290
a fairly low latency
under 2 milliseconds.

00:25:44.290 --> 00:25:46.080
So you're able to
see the ability

00:25:46.080 --> 00:25:49.650
to monitor the performance
as your environment workload

00:25:49.650 --> 00:25:50.500
changes.

00:25:50.500 --> 00:25:52.262
So now let's actually
add some capacity.

00:25:52.262 --> 00:25:53.970
So here we're going
back to that same UI.

00:25:53.970 --> 00:25:56.094
I'm going to add 3 terabytes
of the type of storage

00:25:56.094 --> 00:25:56.800
I've selected.

00:25:56.800 --> 00:25:59.220
So in this case, that's going
to correspond to spinning up

00:25:59.220 --> 00:26:01.050
one additional node.

00:26:01.050 --> 00:26:02.700
So as we saw before,
that's happening

00:26:02.700 --> 00:26:04.260
in the back end on GCP.

00:26:04.260 --> 00:26:06.360
We're spinning up that
additional infrastructure

00:26:06.360 --> 00:26:08.190
to supply that
additional storage.

00:26:08.190 --> 00:26:10.260
So you'll be able to see
now, that node is now

00:26:10.260 --> 00:26:11.910
active and available
in our cluster--

00:26:11.910 --> 00:26:13.964
or it's ready to
deploy in our cluster.

00:26:13.964 --> 00:26:15.630
And we're going to
deploy it, and that's

00:26:15.630 --> 00:26:17.580
going to make it active.

00:26:17.580 --> 00:26:20.940
So once we hit Deploy, now
seamlessly, in the background,

00:26:20.940 --> 00:26:23.384
that additional capacity is
being added into our cluster.

00:26:23.384 --> 00:26:25.300
You don't need to take
any additional actions.

00:26:25.300 --> 00:26:27.390
Your cluster
performance and capacity

00:26:27.390 --> 00:26:29.700
are going to extend with
those additional resources

00:26:29.700 --> 00:26:32.120
seamlessly, simply by
deploying it and adding it.

00:26:32.120 --> 00:26:34.140
And as you saw there,
it's now active.

00:26:34.140 --> 00:26:36.480
And now we have four storage
nodes in our cluster,

00:26:36.480 --> 00:26:39.330
giving us additional capacity
and additional performance.

00:26:39.330 --> 00:26:41.580
So we've actually done quite
a few things here, right?

00:26:41.580 --> 00:26:43.470
We started with some
data on-premises

00:26:43.470 --> 00:26:45.210
that we wanted to
get into the cloud.

00:26:45.210 --> 00:26:47.860
We spun up the Elastifile
file system seamlessly.

00:26:47.860 --> 00:26:50.630
So we had that storage
capacity available there.

00:26:50.630 --> 00:26:52.860
We pushed the data over
into object storage.

00:26:52.860 --> 00:26:55.140
We decided, OK, we're ready
to load the file system,

00:26:55.140 --> 00:26:57.810
so we pulled the data out of
object into the file system.

00:26:57.810 --> 00:26:59.674
We scaled our WordPress
infrastructure.

00:26:59.674 --> 00:27:01.590
And then we scaled our
storage infrastructure.

00:27:01.590 --> 00:27:03.930
So we've done quite a few things
in this kind of simple use case

00:27:03.930 --> 00:27:04.740
example.

00:27:04.740 --> 00:27:08.250
But really, storage and data
management for Kubernetes

00:27:08.250 --> 00:27:11.320
are not topics that only
relate to web services.

00:27:11.320 --> 00:27:13.830
They relate to a much broader
range of applications and use

00:27:13.830 --> 00:27:14.580
cases.

00:27:14.580 --> 00:27:16.990
And with that in mind, I'll
now hand over to Katreena

00:27:16.990 --> 00:27:18.340
from HudsonAlpha,
who's going to take you

00:27:18.340 --> 00:27:20.673
through a different use case
in the scientific computing

00:27:20.673 --> 00:27:21.316
domain.

00:27:21.316 --> 00:27:22.274
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE] ?

00:27:24.551 --> 00:27:26.300
JEROME MCFARLAND: Not
in this example, no.

00:27:26.300 --> 00:27:28.611
But you can use stateful sess
in conjunction with this.

00:27:28.611 --> 00:27:30.610
KATREENA MULLICAN: All
right, think you, Jerome.

00:27:30.610 --> 00:27:31.660
It's great to be here.

00:27:31.660 --> 00:27:32.290
Hi, everyone.

00:27:32.290 --> 00:27:34.630
I am Katreena Mullican,
technology director and cloud

00:27:34.630 --> 00:27:38.420
whisperer at HudsonAlpha.

00:27:38.420 --> 00:27:40.710
HudsonAlpha Institute
for Biotechnology

00:27:40.710 --> 00:27:44.100
is a nonprofit genomic and
genetic research institute

00:27:44.100 --> 00:27:47.070
located in Huntsville, Alabama.

00:27:47.070 --> 00:27:51.390
We leverage the power of
the human genome sequence

00:27:51.390 --> 00:27:54.840
to better understand
causes and early indicators

00:27:54.840 --> 00:27:59.520
for complex diseases, such
as cancer and Alzheimer's,

00:27:59.520 --> 00:28:02.310
and the genetic causes of
rare and undiagnosed diseases

00:28:02.310 --> 00:28:03.510
as well.

00:28:03.510 --> 00:28:06.750
We are a genomic
sequencing leading facility

00:28:06.750 --> 00:28:12.960
in the world for human health as
well as agriscience endeavors.

00:28:12.960 --> 00:28:15.090
Researchers, entrepreneurs,
and educators

00:28:15.090 --> 00:28:19.080
collaborate with each other
and over 40 associate companies

00:28:19.080 --> 00:28:23.000
located on our 160-acre
campus in Huntsville.

00:28:23.000 --> 00:28:28.900
Our education program provides
on-prem and virtual curriculum

00:28:28.900 --> 00:28:33.560
for adults and students, their
teachers, and lifelong learners

00:28:33.560 --> 00:28:34.790
around the world.

00:28:34.790 --> 00:28:39.260
And HudsonAlpha's also home to
the first standalone genomic

00:28:39.260 --> 00:28:42.590
medicine clinic which uses
genome sequencing data

00:28:42.590 --> 00:28:45.372
to provide patient diagnoses.

00:28:45.372 --> 00:28:47.080
So that's a little
bit about HudsonAlpha.

00:28:51.300 --> 00:28:53.190
One of the great things
about HudsonAlpha

00:28:53.190 --> 00:28:56.880
is our ability to rapidly
adopt emerging technologies.

00:28:56.880 --> 00:29:00.570
And to do this, we, in IT,
have a devops mentality

00:29:00.570 --> 00:29:03.120
and treat
infrastructure as code.

00:29:03.120 --> 00:29:06.000
So our on-prem,
composable infrastructure

00:29:06.000 --> 00:29:09.040
provides the backbone for
a variety of use cases,

00:29:09.040 --> 00:29:12.060
including the one shown here,
which is an on-prem Kubernetes

00:29:12.060 --> 00:29:14.970
cluster built on bare metal.

00:29:14.970 --> 00:29:18.090
The Kubernetes master
and several worker nodes

00:29:18.090 --> 00:29:21.240
are provisioned using
open-source tools,

00:29:21.240 --> 00:29:25.620
such as CentOS, and Python,
and HashiCorp Consul.

00:29:25.620 --> 00:29:28.350
Consul provides
service discovery.

00:29:28.350 --> 00:29:31.200
And Datadog, which is a
cloud-hosted monitoring

00:29:31.200 --> 00:29:35.660
service, provides information
about our uptime and monitoring

00:29:35.660 --> 00:29:39.780
application and
infrastructure performance.

00:29:39.780 --> 00:29:41.580
So what we're going
to do today is

00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:43.860
talk about the power
of provisioning

00:29:43.860 --> 00:29:46.200
this on-prem Kubernetes
cluster as code

00:29:46.200 --> 00:29:52.050
and then show why and how
it can become a hybrid cloud

00:29:52.050 --> 00:29:57.160
Kubernetes cluster by
extending onto GCP.

00:29:57.160 --> 00:30:00.240
So first, let's take a look
at the process of provisioning

00:30:00.240 --> 00:30:04.360
this on-prem cluster.

00:30:04.360 --> 00:30:06.140
[LAUGHS] That's really tall.

00:30:06.140 --> 00:30:06.640
OK.

00:30:06.640 --> 00:30:08.860
So here, you'll see some Python.

00:30:08.860 --> 00:30:13.480
So this is just a way to provide
configuration for the master

00:30:13.480 --> 00:30:14.510
and worker nodes here.

00:30:14.510 --> 00:30:16.259
You can see things
like enclosure numbers,

00:30:16.259 --> 00:30:17.720
bay numbers, template names.

00:30:17.720 --> 00:30:19.720
These are all custom
attributes that

00:30:19.720 --> 00:30:25.890
are read by scripts that
work as the nodes come up

00:30:25.890 --> 00:30:28.620
for the first time and provide
a personalization experience.

00:30:28.620 --> 00:30:32.010
So the nodes know their role,
what they're supposed to do.

00:30:32.010 --> 00:30:33.450
Consul plays in here also.

00:30:33.450 --> 00:30:35.700
The roles can read
key-value pairs

00:30:35.700 --> 00:30:37.240
and write key-value pairs.

00:30:37.240 --> 00:30:41.400
So awareness of other nodes
happens through this process.

00:30:41.400 --> 00:30:44.790
There's a simple, common
provisioning script

00:30:44.790 --> 00:30:47.700
that is used for all
of our use cases.

00:30:47.700 --> 00:30:52.100
You'll see it makes a connection
to OneView, in this case,

00:30:52.100 --> 00:30:55.290
for our composable
infrastructure.

00:30:55.290 --> 00:30:58.010
It parses the configuration
from the master and the worker

00:30:58.010 --> 00:31:05.630
files, creates server profiles,
sets custom attributes

00:31:05.630 --> 00:31:07.640
that will be read by
those startup scripts.

00:31:07.640 --> 00:31:10.490
And so the whole point
here is, one line of code,

00:31:10.490 --> 00:31:12.019
and the environment's
provisioned.

00:31:12.019 --> 00:31:13.310
You write this script one time.

00:31:13.310 --> 00:31:15.740
You check it in to
version control.

00:31:15.740 --> 00:31:18.560
And this speaks highly
to accuracy and speed

00:31:18.560 --> 00:31:20.370
of delivering the resources.

00:31:20.370 --> 00:31:22.980
So with this one line right
here, I'm going to go ahead

00:31:22.980 --> 00:31:24.950
and provision a
Kubernetes master node.

00:31:27.987 --> 00:31:29.820
All right, it's already
starting to come up.

00:31:29.820 --> 00:31:31.130
So let's take a look at Consul.

00:31:31.130 --> 00:31:35.260
Consul runs as a
Docker container--

00:31:35.260 --> 00:31:36.260
see, right here.

00:31:36.260 --> 00:31:38.860
So in addition to
key-value pairs,

00:31:38.860 --> 00:31:42.980
it also provide service
discovery, and IP address host

00:31:42.980 --> 00:31:48.160
name, and status of
every member that

00:31:48.160 --> 00:31:49.540
talks to this Consul server.

00:31:49.540 --> 00:31:50.960
So right now, I
just have myself.

00:31:50.960 --> 00:31:52.780
But as things are
provisioned, this

00:31:52.780 --> 00:31:55.152
is a one-stop shop for me
to come type this command,

00:31:55.152 --> 00:31:57.610
see what's in my environment,
and be able to connect to it.

00:32:01.150 --> 00:32:03.490
All right, looks like
this one just powered on.

00:32:03.490 --> 00:32:04.960
That's the master.

00:32:04.960 --> 00:32:06.860
So we're going to
rerun this same script,

00:32:06.860 --> 00:32:09.010
but this time create a worker.

00:32:09.010 --> 00:32:11.710
And I can repeat this for
as many resources as I have.

00:32:11.710 --> 00:32:13.180
I can keep coming
up with workers.

00:32:13.180 --> 00:32:15.640
And we do, of course,
have multiple workers

00:32:15.640 --> 00:32:16.640
in our cluster.

00:32:19.409 --> 00:32:20.950
So we're waiting
for that to come up.

00:32:20.950 --> 00:32:22.860
I'm going to go back
to Consul and actually

00:32:22.860 --> 00:32:28.190
check on things here, see
if the master is showing up.

00:32:28.190 --> 00:32:30.289
Looks like the
worker just came on.

00:32:30.289 --> 00:32:31.080
There's our master.

00:32:34.670 --> 00:32:36.610
And we'll see the
worker in a moment.

00:32:36.610 --> 00:32:39.440
So one of the things
that the automation does

00:32:39.440 --> 00:32:43.577
is it embeds SSH keys,
which allows me to do

00:32:43.577 --> 00:32:44.660
a passwordless connection.

00:32:44.660 --> 00:32:46.420
As soon as the node's
up, as soon as I verify

00:32:46.420 --> 00:32:48.711
it's up with Consul, I can
do a passwordless connection

00:32:48.711 --> 00:32:50.090
into the Kubernetes master.

00:32:50.090 --> 00:32:52.040
So remember, the
only thing I've typed

00:32:52.040 --> 00:32:54.770
is a Python script
with an argument.

00:32:54.770 --> 00:32:57.240
I mean, I have not done any
Kubernetes setup at this point.

00:32:57.240 --> 00:33:01.570
This is all automagic part
of the provisioning scripts.

00:33:01.570 --> 00:33:07.777
So here we come into
the Kubernetes master.

00:33:07.777 --> 00:33:09.610
You're going to do some
sanity checks to see

00:33:09.610 --> 00:33:10.776
if it knows about the nodes.

00:33:10.776 --> 00:33:12.907
And it knows about the master.

00:33:12.907 --> 00:33:13.990
It looks like the worker--

00:33:13.990 --> 00:33:15.550
it's been 20 seconds--

00:33:15.550 --> 00:33:16.640
it's not quite ready.

00:33:16.640 --> 00:33:20.340
So I'm going to give it
just another couple seconds

00:33:20.340 --> 00:33:23.250
and check back.

00:33:23.250 --> 00:33:25.437
There it is.

00:33:25.437 --> 00:33:27.770
Another thing that the automatic
scripts do in this case

00:33:27.770 --> 00:33:30.380
is they embed Datadog
monitoring as a daemon set.

00:33:30.380 --> 00:33:32.530
So that's the next thing
I'm going to check on

00:33:32.530 --> 00:33:34.230
is to see if this happened.

00:33:34.230 --> 00:33:38.170
It'll automatically deploy a
daemon set per worker node.

00:33:38.170 --> 00:33:39.550
And of course, Datadog was--

00:33:39.550 --> 00:33:42.280
like I mentioned, it's
used for monitoring

00:33:42.280 --> 00:33:45.110
of uptime and performance.

00:33:45.110 --> 00:33:49.712
So we see we do have a Datadog
agent running on our worker.

00:33:49.712 --> 00:33:52.170
So what I'm going to do, just
for the purpose of this demo,

00:33:52.170 --> 00:33:55.560
is just an nginx
deployment to show you--

00:33:55.560 --> 00:33:57.380
we're going to start
with three replicas,

00:33:57.380 --> 00:34:03.070
so I can scale it up
later and see how this

00:34:03.070 --> 00:34:07.300
works in just a little bit.

00:34:07.300 --> 00:34:11.370
So starting with three,
just your typical nginx--

00:34:11.370 --> 00:34:14.460
this is sanity checking
for the environment

00:34:14.460 --> 00:34:19.115
to make sure that my
cluster and API are working.

00:34:25.380 --> 00:34:26.100
All right.

00:34:26.100 --> 00:34:30.000
And just as we expect, we
have containers creating.

00:34:30.000 --> 00:34:31.830
And there's our pods.

00:34:31.830 --> 00:34:36.469
So, so far, so good.

00:34:36.469 --> 00:34:39.170
So this is all
on-prem, bare metal,

00:34:39.170 --> 00:34:44.020
Kubernetes provisioned as code
on composable infrastructure

00:34:44.020 --> 00:34:45.130
at HudsonAlpha.

00:34:45.130 --> 00:34:47.199
And as a final check,
we'll just check on Datadog

00:34:47.199 --> 00:34:48.272
while we're at it.

00:34:48.272 --> 00:34:49.730
Yes, those daemon
sets are running,

00:34:49.730 --> 00:34:51.340
so I should have my
master and worker.

00:34:51.340 --> 00:34:53.147
And I should be
starting to gather--

00:34:53.147 --> 00:34:54.730
it just is now coming
up, but starting

00:34:54.730 --> 00:34:57.520
to gather these metrics.

00:34:57.520 --> 00:35:02.762
But so what if the on-prem
environment is not enough?

00:35:02.762 --> 00:35:04.970
You know, genomics takes a
tremendous amount of data.

00:35:04.970 --> 00:35:09.000
We generate about five
petabytes of data each year.

00:35:09.000 --> 00:35:13.660
And one of the tricks
with running in-cloud

00:35:13.660 --> 00:35:16.440
is, how do you get your
data into the cloud?

00:35:16.440 --> 00:35:18.640
So as Jerome
mentioned, Elastifile

00:35:18.640 --> 00:35:21.050
is helping us do that.

00:35:21.050 --> 00:35:23.600
The amount of compute
can sometimes not

00:35:23.600 --> 00:35:25.940
be sufficient in your
on-prem environment either.

00:35:25.940 --> 00:35:28.980
So let me just give you
a genomic sequencing 101.

00:35:28.980 --> 00:35:31.530
When DNA and genomes
are sequenced,

00:35:31.530 --> 00:35:33.000
that's represented as data.

00:35:33.000 --> 00:35:35.660
So you take the data
from a sequence subject,

00:35:35.660 --> 00:35:38.600
you compare it to what we
call a reference genome.

00:35:38.600 --> 00:35:40.520
And the process of
figuring out all

00:35:40.520 --> 00:35:43.190
of the differences between
you and a reference genome

00:35:43.190 --> 00:35:44.720
is known as variant calling.

00:35:44.720 --> 00:35:47.090
It's a very
compute-intensive process.

00:35:47.090 --> 00:35:48.860
Requires a lot of
scratch space and a lot

00:35:48.860 --> 00:35:50.930
of demand on your resources.

00:35:50.930 --> 00:35:53.440
And it's accomplished
through a set of--

00:35:53.440 --> 00:35:55.490
what we call a
pipeline, a set of tools

00:35:55.490 --> 00:35:57.290
that execute in a certain order.

00:35:57.290 --> 00:36:00.110
What happens, though, is that
new versions of the pipeline

00:36:00.110 --> 00:36:00.990
become available.

00:36:00.990 --> 00:36:05.930
So we size our data center to
handle the day-to-day, 24/7

00:36:05.930 --> 00:36:07.310
variant calling, right?

00:36:07.310 --> 00:36:10.400
But then when these
new versions come out,

00:36:10.400 --> 00:36:12.076
researchers want to go back.

00:36:12.076 --> 00:36:13.700
In addition to the
day-to-day workload,

00:36:13.700 --> 00:36:15.350
they want to go back
and reanalyze everything

00:36:15.350 --> 00:36:16.225
with the new version.

00:36:16.225 --> 00:36:18.470
Because they might glean
some new knowledge,

00:36:18.470 --> 00:36:20.180
because this new
version has come out.

00:36:20.180 --> 00:36:23.630
So in that case, your
computer requirements

00:36:23.630 --> 00:36:28.230
are definitely stressed
beyond their normal workload.

00:36:28.230 --> 00:36:30.570
The good news is that
tools and pipelines

00:36:30.570 --> 00:36:33.270
are becoming containerized.

00:36:33.270 --> 00:36:36.519
And therefore, a
great solution--

00:36:36.519 --> 00:36:38.560
and this is what I set
out to do at HudsonAlpha--

00:36:38.560 --> 00:36:43.140
was to figure out how to
burst our on-prem Kubernetes

00:36:43.140 --> 00:36:46.400
solution into public cloud.

00:36:46.400 --> 00:36:48.500
And part of what
makes that possible

00:36:48.500 --> 00:36:52.640
is things like a direct
connect, VPN to public cloud.

00:36:52.640 --> 00:36:55.397
There are ways to
connect the networks,

00:36:55.397 --> 00:36:57.730
but you also have to worry
about connecting the storage.

00:36:57.730 --> 00:37:00.380
So the on-prem worker nodes
access the on-prem data.

00:37:00.380 --> 00:37:03.950
The cloud worker nodes
access the cloud data.

00:37:03.950 --> 00:37:06.920
So for us, that's a
matter of deploying

00:37:06.920 --> 00:37:10.400
Elastifile in the cloud
and CloudConnect on-prem.

00:37:10.400 --> 00:37:12.150
I'll show a graphic
in just a moment.

00:37:12.150 --> 00:37:14.810
But that was the next
hurdle to overcome

00:37:14.810 --> 00:37:17.660
is, how are we going to get the
appropriate data into the cloud

00:37:17.660 --> 00:37:20.660
so that these worker
nodes can chew on that?

00:37:23.290 --> 00:37:24.290
The good news is Consul.

00:37:24.290 --> 00:37:26.164
And then what I'm using
for service discovery

00:37:26.164 --> 00:37:28.060
can communicate with
everybody because

00:37:28.060 --> 00:37:33.340
of that network, the private
networking setup between us

00:37:33.340 --> 00:37:33.964
and the cloud.

00:37:33.964 --> 00:37:35.380
And then, of course,
your workload

00:37:35.380 --> 00:37:37.300
could be manually
scaled, or ideally,

00:37:37.300 --> 00:37:40.567
auto-scaled as soon as those
resources become available.

00:37:40.567 --> 00:37:41.650
So this is how this looks.

00:37:41.650 --> 00:37:45.335
On the left is
Huntsville, Alabama.

00:37:45.335 --> 00:37:46.960
Here's our on-prem
compute that we just

00:37:46.960 --> 00:37:48.668
talked about with the
master and workers.

00:37:48.668 --> 00:37:51.880
So I showed how easy
that is to spin up.

00:37:51.880 --> 00:37:55.400
Existing NAS or
Elastifile on-prem

00:37:55.400 --> 00:37:57.680
can work with the
CloudConnect node.

00:37:57.680 --> 00:38:00.260
So CloudConnect knows
about our existing data.

00:38:00.260 --> 00:38:05.520
CloudConnect, as Jerome
showed, pushes the data up

00:38:05.520 --> 00:38:09.380
to GCS cloud storage.

00:38:09.380 --> 00:38:14.460
And then over in Google land, we
can push up as many Kubernetes

00:38:14.460 --> 00:38:15.620
workers as we'd like.

00:38:15.620 --> 00:38:17.480
And they all have
access to that data.

00:38:17.480 --> 00:38:21.270
So the workers at the
bottom here, the tool set,

00:38:21.270 --> 00:38:24.270
is very similar, except
I'm going with Terraform

00:38:24.270 --> 00:38:27.340
for my provisioning in public
cloud instead of Python,

00:38:27.340 --> 00:38:31.735
and we've added Elastifile,
of course, to the mix.

00:38:31.735 --> 00:38:32.610
So let's take a look.

00:38:32.610 --> 00:38:34.830
This is my Terraform.

00:38:34.830 --> 00:38:36.690
So it's pretty
standard stuff-- just

00:38:36.690 --> 00:38:38.850
define a Google Cloud provider.

00:38:38.850 --> 00:38:41.250
There's a few variables.

00:38:41.250 --> 00:38:43.100
Of course, at your
discretion, you

00:38:43.100 --> 00:38:46.120
can choose as many variables as
you'd like for the environment.

00:38:46.120 --> 00:38:50.780
This just shows some of
the ones I'm using here.

00:38:50.780 --> 00:38:53.087
And one of the big
differences here

00:38:53.087 --> 00:38:54.920
to accomplish the
automation is that there's

00:38:54.920 --> 00:38:56.990
a scripts and a service
files directory.

00:38:56.990 --> 00:39:00.746
The scripts consist of the--

00:39:00.746 --> 00:39:04.850
they're Bash scripts, but they
start up all of the application

00:39:04.850 --> 00:39:07.280
that we need to and
all of the automation.

00:39:07.280 --> 00:39:09.480
And then the service
files are systemd files.

00:39:09.480 --> 00:39:13.940
So it really is as simple
as telling Terraform

00:39:13.940 --> 00:39:15.080
that we want--

00:39:15.080 --> 00:39:17.340
in this case, I
just chose a number,

00:39:17.340 --> 00:39:19.490
so we're going to say three--

00:39:19.490 --> 00:39:21.197
purely vanilla CentOS 7.

00:39:21.197 --> 00:39:23.030
There's nothing special
about that CentOS 7,

00:39:23.030 --> 00:39:23.946
and that's on purpose.

00:39:23.946 --> 00:39:25.356
I'm a big believer
that you don't

00:39:25.356 --> 00:39:26.730
put the magic into
the image, you

00:39:26.730 --> 00:39:28.370
put the magic into the code.

00:39:28.370 --> 00:39:31.670
So scripts and service
file directories

00:39:31.670 --> 00:39:32.781
are going to be copied up.

00:39:32.781 --> 00:39:34.280
And then these are
commands that are

00:39:34.280 --> 00:39:35.696
going to be executed
automatically

00:39:35.696 --> 00:39:36.950
on each node as they boot.

00:39:36.950 --> 00:39:41.960
Some of this is to mount
Elastifile NFS in fstab,

00:39:41.960 --> 00:39:47.390
to set up my DNS for Elastifile
and to mount that directory.

00:39:47.390 --> 00:39:49.960
So at this point, I've
written the code, pushed it up

00:39:49.960 --> 00:39:51.130
to version control.

00:39:51.130 --> 00:39:55.454
I'm going to do a sanity
check here with Terraform.

00:39:55.454 --> 00:39:57.620
And if everything looks
good, go ahead and apply it.

00:39:57.620 --> 00:40:00.080
And we're going to watch this
thing come to life in Google.

00:40:00.080 --> 00:40:03.290
And then it will
automatically associate itself

00:40:03.290 --> 00:40:08.445
with my on-prem, bare
metal Kubernetes cluster.

00:40:08.445 --> 00:40:10.420
And this is real-time here.

00:40:10.420 --> 00:40:13.590
[LAUGHS] So from the
moment I type "yes,"

00:40:13.590 --> 00:40:17.770
I can immediately
go check out GCP.

00:40:17.770 --> 00:40:23.367
Do a refresh, and
they're coming.

00:40:23.367 --> 00:40:25.533
Here's the three nodes, of
course, that I asked for.

00:40:29.160 --> 00:40:31.000
Go back and shave a
little bit of time off.

00:40:31.000 --> 00:40:32.458
It's been 2 minutes
and 40 seconds.

00:40:32.458 --> 00:40:33.590
Everybody's up.

00:40:33.590 --> 00:40:35.250
And my favorite command--

00:40:35.250 --> 00:40:36.390
are they here?

00:40:36.390 --> 00:40:37.930
Yes.

00:40:37.930 --> 00:40:40.330
I not only have my on-prem
bare metal Kubernetes cluster,

00:40:40.330 --> 00:40:41.830
I've got the three
new worker nodes.

00:40:41.830 --> 00:40:44.540
I've got their IPs,
I've got their status,

00:40:44.540 --> 00:40:45.740
so we're good to go.

00:40:45.740 --> 00:40:48.370
I can connect into one of
these, if I want to for sanity.

00:40:48.370 --> 00:40:50.630
I don't have to.

00:40:50.630 --> 00:40:52.157
I can say, let's
connect to it once.

00:40:52.157 --> 00:40:53.740
Let's just make sure
the scripts work.

00:40:53.740 --> 00:40:55.359
Let's make sure
my data is there.

00:40:55.359 --> 00:40:56.900
So we're going to
pick the first guy.

00:40:56.900 --> 00:41:00.710
We're going to connect to
him and just do a simple df,

00:41:00.710 --> 00:41:04.400
and looking for this line.

00:41:04.400 --> 00:41:05.550
So he's mounted up.

00:41:05.550 --> 00:41:11.600
And at that point, this node
can consume that directory

00:41:11.600 --> 00:41:13.860
as it needs to, if the
app's running on there.

00:41:13.860 --> 00:41:15.240
So we exit back.

00:41:15.240 --> 00:41:17.930
We're going to repeat the
ssh to our worker node--

00:41:17.930 --> 00:41:21.180
I'm sorry, our master node.

00:41:21.180 --> 00:41:23.840
And I'm going to ask
it to get the nodes.

00:41:23.840 --> 00:41:25.660
We're going to see
if everybody's here.

00:41:25.660 --> 00:41:26.650
Yep.

00:41:26.650 --> 00:41:30.180
So I've got my
three GCP workers.

00:41:30.180 --> 00:41:31.690
They're good to go.

00:41:31.690 --> 00:41:34.080
And I'm going to do a
sanity check on Datadog,

00:41:34.080 --> 00:41:36.780
make sure the daemon set's
functioning as it should across

00:41:36.780 --> 00:41:37.460
to the cloud.

00:41:40.060 --> 00:41:45.290
I should have one-- yep, I have
one per GCP node over there.

00:41:45.290 --> 00:41:47.780
And then as a final
demonstration,

00:41:47.780 --> 00:41:51.200
instead of auto-scaling it, I'm
just going to force my nginx

00:41:51.200 --> 00:41:53.499
deployment to go to, like, 30--

00:41:53.499 --> 00:41:55.790
pick a nice number now that
I've got some more compute.

00:41:58.512 --> 00:42:00.470
Yep, I've got three that
I asked for initially.

00:42:00.470 --> 00:42:01.678
We're going to scale that up.

00:42:15.550 --> 00:42:16.570
Sorry about the typing.

00:42:16.570 --> 00:42:17.500
I guess I was a little tired.

00:42:17.500 --> 00:42:18.041
I don't know.

00:42:18.041 --> 00:42:18.910
It's kind of slow.

00:42:18.910 --> 00:42:20.860
I'm working on it.

00:42:20.860 --> 00:42:21.630
OK.

00:42:21.630 --> 00:42:22.630
All right, scaled it up.

00:42:22.630 --> 00:42:25.530
And repeat the pods,
just to show, yeah,

00:42:25.530 --> 00:42:27.730
everybody's at the party now.

00:42:27.730 --> 00:42:30.280
We've got on-prem
from the bare metal.

00:42:30.280 --> 00:42:32.860
We've got the GCP nodes.

00:42:32.860 --> 00:42:35.500
And each of these can
be scaled independently

00:42:35.500 --> 00:42:37.790
based on which script
you're executing.

00:42:37.790 --> 00:42:39.634
If you want more on-prem,
you do the Python.

00:42:39.634 --> 00:42:41.800
If you want more in the
cloud, you do the Terraform.

00:42:41.800 --> 00:42:43.660
But it's all code.

00:42:43.660 --> 00:42:45.610
It works every time.

00:42:45.610 --> 00:42:47.837
And it's repeatable
at this point.

00:42:47.837 --> 00:42:50.170
You don't have to waste a lot
of time with sanity checks

00:42:50.170 --> 00:42:52.030
anymore, because
you know it's going

00:42:52.030 --> 00:42:53.450
to be the same every time.

00:42:53.450 --> 00:42:55.420
So here's Datadog.

00:42:55.420 --> 00:42:57.890
They've checked in,
and I'm starting

00:42:57.890 --> 00:43:00.080
to gather metrics
on those already.

00:43:00.080 --> 00:43:03.440
So that's a successful
scaled Kubernetes cluster

00:43:03.440 --> 00:43:06.020
with my on-prem
environment as one

00:43:06.020 --> 00:43:10.894
of the clouds, my private cloud,
right, and then GCP public.

00:43:10.894 --> 00:43:15.832
So I'll give it back to you.

00:43:15.832 --> 00:43:17.290
JEROME MCFARLAND:
Thanks, Katreena.

00:43:17.290 --> 00:43:20.060
Really cool stuff
to see those types

00:43:20.060 --> 00:43:22.250
of workflows in action
for real-world use cases,

00:43:22.250 --> 00:43:24.370
especially in domains
like life sciences

00:43:24.370 --> 00:43:27.400
that can benefit so many of us.

00:43:27.400 --> 00:43:30.220
Just a couple of final
thoughts or things

00:43:30.220 --> 00:43:33.440
that we want to
leave you with here--

00:43:33.440 --> 00:43:35.460
so if you remember
anything, what

00:43:35.460 --> 00:43:38.950
we'd like you to really remember
is that you have the capability

00:43:38.950 --> 00:43:41.890
to leverage Kubernetes
stateful services

00:43:41.890 --> 00:43:47.020
to package, scale, and mobilize
a broad variety of applications

00:43:47.020 --> 00:43:47.950
and workflows.

00:43:47.950 --> 00:43:50.380
Again, like I
mentioned before, it's

00:43:50.380 --> 00:43:54.340
no longer the sole domain of
microservices and stateless

00:43:54.340 --> 00:43:55.389
applications.

00:43:55.389 --> 00:43:56.930
You can leverage
Kubernetes for much,

00:43:56.930 --> 00:43:58.305
much more, including
applications

00:43:58.305 --> 00:44:01.720
that need to persist, and
manage, and mobilize data.

00:44:01.720 --> 00:44:03.910
We encourage you to evaluate
your storage options

00:44:03.910 --> 00:44:04.780
very carefully.

00:44:04.780 --> 00:44:07.090
You have lots of
options with Kubernetes,

00:44:07.090 --> 00:44:10.000
but make sure that you're really
considering the key selection

00:44:10.000 --> 00:44:13.320
criteria for your workflows
around usability, scalability,

00:44:13.320 --> 00:44:15.596
shareability, and data
mobility to make sure

00:44:15.596 --> 00:44:17.470
that you have a solution
that can provide all

00:44:17.470 --> 00:44:19.420
the needs that
you're going to have,

00:44:19.420 --> 00:44:21.290
both today and in the future.

